celebrating the holy month of ramadan  in the ruins  tariq abu ziyad and his family fasted  from dawn until dusk  and now they're breaking that fast in  what was once  their home a splash of color  in a sea of crushed concrete abusive  says  he fled his home in ariha at the end of  the year when the syrian army and the  russians  bombed this area  now my family and i are here on top of  the destruction  we are reliving a very difficult and  painful memory  i pray that god doesn't let anyone else  experience this  his mother says with the kitchen  destroyed they brought ready meals from  outside  the most important thing is that we  relive our memories and eat in our home  ariha is situated in idlib province a  few kilometers west  of the strategic m5 highway  held for years by rebel forces it was  the major target for the syrian  government  to capture during an offensive in  december  nearly a million people were displaced  by the violence  most fled north arriving at the turkish  border  where many live in makeshift camps  a recent u.n report says in 2019 syria  had the largest number of new  displacements of any country  almost 1.9 million the situation for  displaced people living at the border  was  dire when a ceasefire was agreed between  russia and turkey in march  tens of thousands of people returned to  their homes  some like abusiad's family found them  completely destroyed  but many found it easier to live amongst  the ruins  rather than return to the camps laura  about a manly al jazeera
 

